A database is an organized collection of data that is stored and managed to allow easy access, retrieval, and manipulation. It is used to store structured or unstructured data in a way that ensures efficiency, security, and scalability.

Types of Databases:
Relational Databases (RDBMS) – Data is stored in tables with rows and columns (e.g., MySQL, PostgreSQL, SQL Server, Oracle).
NoSQL Databases – Designed for flexible and scalable data storage (e.g., MongoDB, Cassandra, Redis).
Graph Databases – Store relationships between data in graph structures (e.g., Neo4j).
Time-Series Databases – Optimized for time-stamped data (e.g., InfluxDB, TimescaleDB).
Key-Value Stores – Simple databases that use key-value pairs (e.g., Redis, DynamoDB).
Document-Oriented Databases – Store data as documents (e.g., MongoDB, CouchDB).
Key Database Concepts:
Tables, Rows, and Columns (for RDBMS)
Schemas – Defines the structure of a database.
Indexes – Improve search speed.
Queries – SQL (Structured Query Language) is used for retrieving data.
Transactions – Ensure data integrity using ACID properties (Atomicity, Consistency, Isolation, Durability).
